#pagebreak(weak: true)
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[QUESTIONS FOR REFLECTION])
#v(0.1in)

#set enum(spacing: 0.9em, indent: 0pt, body-indent: 0.6em)

+ "Restless is our heart until it comes to rest in thee" (I.1.1). Where do
  you notice restlessness in your own life, and where do you look for rest?

+ Augustine writes the whole book as a prayer, to God rather than about
  him. What changes when you tell your story to God instead of about
  yourself?

+ He sees selfishness even in a jealous baby (I.7.11). Do you agree that
  sin begins so early? What do you make of his honesty about it?

+ He loved weeping over Dido more than over his own soul (I.13.20-21).
  What stories move you more easily than your own condition does?

+ He was beaten at school, and later says he learned more from freedom
  than from fear (I.14.23). What do you take from that as a teacher or a
  student?

+ The book ends in thanks for small gifts: being, life, sense, memory
  (I.20.31). Make your own list.
